<h1>From Pilots to Profit:<br>How to Gain Real Value from AI</h1>
<p>Many organizations have successful AI pilots but no impact on profit. This is not a technology problem. It is an organizational readiness problem — and it can be de-risked.</p>
<div class="quote">“We have invested heavily in AI. Every use case feels like starting from scratch. We win battles but lose the war — we cannot turn experiments into a capability that drives customer value.”</div>
<p style="font-size:.95rem;opacity:.85">Reading time: ~10 minutes. Part A defines status & direction. Part B shows how to start change safely.</p>
</div></div>

<nav class="toc"><div>
<a href="#gap">1 Gap</a><a href="#error">2 Error</a><a href="#blockers">3 Self-test</a><a href="#readiness">4 Readiness</a><a href="#engine">5 Engine</a><a href="#proof">6 Proof</a><a href="#path">7 Path</a><a href="#start">8 Start</a>
</div></nav>

<main>
<section id="gap"><div class="kicker">Part A — Why</div><h2>1. Adoption is not Impact</h2>
<p>78% use AI in at least one function. Over 80% see no enterprise-level profit impact. Adoption goes up, strategic impact stays flat.</p>
<div class="formula">AI Adoption <span>≠</span> Impact</div>
<p><b>High performers do it differently:</b> they use AI for growth, not just cost. AI is central to strategy, teams are restructured, people are reskilled, humans + AI work as a team.</p>
<div class="grid2"><div class="mini"><b>Doing things fast and cheap</b><br>Minimize waste. Efficiency.</div><div class="mini"><b>Doing things that create value</b><br>Maximize effectiveness. Impact.</div></div>
<p><b>Takeaway:</b> AI only creates value if you design the organization to allow it.</p></section>

<section id="error"><div class="kicker">Why we fail</div><h2>2. The strategic error</h2>
<div class="formula">Technical Installation <span>≠</span> AI Readiness</div>
<p>Installing a tech stack does not make you ready. It creates an expensive, secure capability that does not fit business needs — siloed in IT, disconnected from profit & loss, optimizing single tasks instead of end-to-end flow.</p>
<p>Legacy thinking creates siloed departments. Siloed departments create fragmented systems. Fragmented systems reinforce rigid thinking. Fixing systems will not fix thinking. Start with thinking, then give it structure to scale.</p></section>

<section id="blockers"><div class="kicker">What — Where do you stand?</div><h2>3. The 7 blockers: interactive self-test</h2>
<p>Score each: <b>1 = Very true for us (blocked)</b> to <b>5 = Not true (ready)</b>. Be honest. Most organizations recognize 3 or more. That is normal.</p>

<div id="tests"></div>
<div id="result"></div>
<p style="font-size:.9rem;color:var(--muted)">Your score stays in your browser. No data is sent.</p></section>

<section id="readiness"><div class="kicker">What readiness really means</div><h2>4. AI Readiness = Readiness of Structures</h2>
<p>Readiness is designed outside-in, starting from customer need — not from IT capabilities.</p>
<div class="arch">
<div>Customer Need / Customer Path</div>
<div>Value Creation (end-to-end flow)</div>
<div>Business Capability / Task to be done</div>
<div>AI / Components / Applications</div>
<div class="op">Operating Model — connects all layers, enables dialogue & learning</div>
<div>DATA • CLOUD • SECURITY as shared enablers</div>
</div>
<p><b>Rule:</b> Enable vertically, deliver horizontally. Architecture must be pulled by service demand, not pushed by technology.</p></section>

<section id="engine"><div class="kicker">What good looks like</div><h2>5. The Value Creation Engine</h2>
<p>AI becomes part of normal solution creation, not an add-on layer. Service Area Teams own their value stream end-to-end (Business + AI + App). Platform Teams enable them (data, infra, security).</p>
<div class="engine">
<div class="path A"><b>Path A — Standardized</b><br>Predictable work. Goal: efficiency. Fixed teams, SOPs. AI for forecasting, automation.</div>
<div class="path B"><b>Path B — Iterative</b><br>Unpredictable work. Goal: learn fast. Self-organizing teams. AI for GenAI, agents, new discovery.</div>
</div>
<p>All demand goes through one router. Every model is monitored for accuracy, impact and ROI. Sense & Respond loop triggers learning or retraining. Governance is built-in, not a gate.</p></section>

<section id="proof"><div class="kicker">Proof it works</div><h2>6. A generic story in 60 seconds</h2>
<p>A mid-size service organization. Knowledge work, many customers, pressure on price.</p>
<table class="case-table"><tr><th>Before</th><th>Change</th><th>After</th></tr>
<tr><td>Silos, slow offers, declining win-rate, utilization 65%, profit ~3%</td><td>Grouped around value into Service Areas. One homebase per person + freedom to choose work. One platform to route demand to Path A or B.</td><td>Offer time -70%, win-rate +50%, utilization ~90%, profit 3% → 15%</td></tr></table>
<p><b>Biggest learning:</b> AI became accurate only when human context was added — experience, successes, feelings, customer culture — not just CVs and tickets. <i>Readiness is organization design.</i></p></section>

<section id="path"><div class="kicker">Part B — How</div><h2>7. Courage to start: KickStart, then Build the System</h2>
<div class="grid2">
<div class="mini"><b>KICKSTART — 3-6 months</b><br>Validate & de-risk.<br>• Diagnose architecture + data<br>• Pick 1 domain with real P&L pain<br>• Build 1-2 end-to-end pilots<br>• Red-team for safety<br><b>Outcome:</b> evidence-based business case + playbook + readiness map. Go / No-Go.</div>
<div class="mini"><b>BUILD THE SYSTEM — 6-18 months</b><br>Operationalize & scale.<br>• Design target architecture from learnings<br>• Form integrated Service Area Teams<br>• Establish operating rhythm<br>• Scale to other streams<br><b>Outcome:</b> AI as capability, not project.</div>
</div>
<p><b>Narrative shift:</b> From building a platform (no immediate value) to building a capability (solves a business problem).</p></section>

<section id="start"><div class="kicker">Call to action</div><h2>8. Start with a diagnostic</h2>
<div class="cta"><h3 style="margin:0">Technology is ready. Are we ready to let the organization learn?</h3>
<p>No big bang. Start safe:</p>
<p>1. 2-3 week diagnostic on the 7 blockers &nbsp;•&nbsp; 2. Agree one high-value problem + sponsor &nbsp;•&nbsp; 3. KickStart it</p>
<a href="#">Ask for a Diagnostic & KickStart</a></div>
<p style="margin-top:14px"><b>5 imperatives to remember:</b> 1) Mindset first 2) Architecture is destiny 3) Integrate holistically 4) Context over code 5) Act on evidence, no big bang.</p></section>

<details><summary>Appendix for Architects (click to expand)</summary>
<p><b>Dual operating logic:</b> Standardized = efficiency, utilization, doing things right. Iterative = efficacy, speed of learning, doing the right things.</p>
<p><b>Data principle:</b> Data as product, owned where created. Collect data that drives decisions, not everything. Context-rich, not raw.</p>
<p><b>Methods used in KickStart:</b> Service catalog analysis, value stream mapping, business architecture assessment, use-case prioritization, service blueprint, launch-and-learn pilots.</p>
<p><b>Methods in Build System:</b> Service-based architecture design, platform/MLOps design, adaptable operating model, communities of learning, sense & respond metrics (TVO, ROCE, CX).</p>
</details>

</main>
<footer>Draft V1 skeleton — simplified for discussion. No vendor branding. Concept logic kept in background. Next: refine wording, visuals, and interactive scoring thresholds together.</footer>

<script>
const blockers=[
["1. Legacy labyrinth","Touching one system breaks another. Integrations are brittle.","Wrap legacy in clear service boundaries so AI can connect without breaking the core."],
["2. Data without context","Data owned nowhere, lost in transfer. AI gets noise.","Own data where it is created. Treat data as product with context."],
["3. Pilot purgatory","Tech pilots disconnected from P&L, never scale.","Require a P&L pain + end-to-end slice before building."],
["4. Rigid governance","Governance as gate between steps, causes delay.","Embed safety & ethics in teams. Secure-by-design, enable value."],
["5. Spot optimization","One task faster, whole flow still stuck.","Map customer path first. Apply AI to bottlenecks."],
["6. Cost fixation","AI seen only as cost cutter (TCO).","Shift to Total Value (TVO): growth, ROCE, experience."],
["7. Model drift","Models treated as static software.","Treat as living agents. Monitor + retrain in loop."]
];
const c=document.getElementById('tests');
blockers.forEach((b,i)=>{
 const d=document.createElement('div');d.className='blocker';
 d.innerHTML=`<h3>${b[0]}</h3><p>${b[1]}</p><div class="fix"><b>Fix →</b> ${b[2]}</div><div class="score-row">${[1,2,3,4,5].map(v=>`<label><input type="radio" name="b${i}" value="${v}"><span>${v}</span></label>`).join('')}</div>`;
 c.appendChild(d);
});
c.addEventListener('change',()=>{
 const vals=[...document.querySelectorAll('#tests input:checked')].map(e=>+e.value);
 if(vals.length<7) return;
 const sum=vals.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0);const avg=(sum/7).toFixed(1);
 const r=document.getElementById('result');r.style.display='block';
 let msg= avg<=2.2?"Low readiness — normal. Start with diagnostic + 1 focused KickStart. Do not scale yet.": avg<=3.5?"Mixed readiness — pick one domain where data + ownership are closest, prove value there.":"Relatively ready — use KickStart to prove TVO, then systematize to other areas.";
 r.innerHTML=`<b>Your average: ${avg} / 5 (total ${sum}/35)</b><br>${msg}`;
 r.scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth',block:'nearest'});
});
</script>
